        },
        "faq_1": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What is the GHS-R1a receptor?",
            "answer": "<p>GHS-R1a is the growth-hormone secretagogue receptor, commonly called the ghrelin receptor. It is a signalling receptor involved in neuroendocrine and metabolic research.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_2": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How does Ipamorelin interact with the ghrelin receptor?",
            "answer": "<p>Mechanistic studies examine Ipamorelin binding to and activating GHS-R1a. The resulting intracellular signalling can be associated with pituitary GH release in experimental systems.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_3": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How is Ipamorelin studied in growth-hormone research?",
            "answer": "<p>Researchers may evaluate receptor activity, the timing and magnitude of pulsatile GH release, and downstream somatotropic markers. Results vary by model, design and evidence quality.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_4": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Is Ipamorelin the same as growth hormone?",
            "answer": "<p>No. Ipamorelin is a receptor agonist studied for stimulating endogenous GH release; it is not growth hormone administered directly.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_5": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Is Ipamorelin a GHRH analogue?",
            "answer": "<p>No. Ipamorelin is studied through GHS-R1a, whereas GHRH analogues act through the GHRH receptor.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_6": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How does Ipamorelin differ from CJC-1295?",
            "answer": "<p>Ipamorelin is associated with the ghrelin-receptor pathway. CJC-1295 is associated with the GHRH pathway, so they do not act on the same receptor.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_7": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What is the relationship between Ipamorelin, GH and IGF-1?",
            "answer": "<p>GHS-R1a activation may stimulate experimental GH release, which can influence downstream IGF-1 signalling. That sequence is indirect and depends on the biological model and study conditions.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_8": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Does growth-hormone release automatically indicate muscle growth?",
            "answer": "<p>No. A measured increase in GH release does not by itself demonstrate increased muscle mass. Body-composition outcomes require separate, appropriately controlled evidence.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_9": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What does the reported selectivity of Ipamorelin mean?",
            "answer": "<p>It refers to pharmacology findings suggesting a more focused GH-secretagogue response than some earlier compounds. It does not establish safety, efficacy or an approved therapeutic use.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_10": {
          "type": "faq",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How should lyophilized Ipamorelin be stored for research?",
            "answer": "<p>Keep the sealed lyophilized material refrigerated at 2–8°C, protected from light and moisture, and follow the batch documentation and laboratory handling requirements.</p>"
          }
        },
